    <article class="content-section">
        
        <h2>The Problem</h2>
        <p>Long-distance relationships suffer from a lack of non-verbal cues. Text messaging often conceals negative emotions, leading to misunderstandings and a sense of disconnection.</p>
        
        <h2>The Solution</h2>
        <p>Squida is an IoT-enabled lamp that sits on the user's desk. It allows partners to send "ambient" emotional signals (colors) without the pressure of composing a text message.</p>
        <img src="path/to/squida-hero.jpg" class="project-img" alt="Squida Final Render">
        <p class="caption">Final rendering of the Squida device.</p>

        <h2>Research & Insights</h2>
        <p>We interviewed 5 couples currently in LDRs. Key insights included:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Concealed Negativity:</strong> Partners hide bad days to avoid "burdening" the other.</li>
            <li><strong>Time Zone Friction:</strong> Real-time calls are rare; asynchronous signals are needed.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Ideation & Prototyping</h2>
        <p>We started with sketches focusing on organic shapes that felt "friendly" rather than "techy."</p>
        <img src="path/to/sketches.jpg" class="project-img" alt="Initial Sketches">
        <p>Moved to SolidWorks for the enclosure design, ensuring it could house the Arduino components.</p>

        <h2>Outcome & Reflection</h2>
        <p>The final prototype was successfully tested for ergonomic fit. If I were to do this again, I would focus more on the mobile app interface that controls the lamp colors.</p>

    </article>

